Let's talk about a few of these because the mechanisms are actually wild. Brazil nuts are so selenium-dense because the trees pull the mineral from selenium-rich soil and concentrate it in the nut — which is exactly why just 1-2 a day is the max most people should eat, more than that and you risk overshooting your needs. Mushrooms work differently: they contain a compound called ergosterol that converts to vitamin D2 when hit with UV light, basically turning your countertop into a mini solar panel for 10-15 minutes. And sardines get their calcium boost from their soft, edible bones, which is why canned sardines often beat fresh fish nutritionally. Everyone's body and needs are different, so treat this as a starting point, not a prescription.

